{"experiments":[{"experimentNo":1,"title":"Explore microcontrollers based programs using Embedded C.","aim":"To familiarize with the microcontroller programming environment and write basic programs using Embedded C to control General Purpose Input/Output (GPIO) pins.","objectives":"- To understand the syntax and structure of Embedded C programs.
- To learn the process of compiling, linking, and flashing a program onto a microcontroller.
- To gain practical experience with controlling digital output pins on a microcontroller.","theory":"Embedded C is a set of language extensions for the C programming language by the C Standards Committee to address commonality issues that exist between C extensions for different embedded processors. The core differences from standard C include the support for fixed-point arithmetic, named address spaces, and basic I/O register access. Microcontrollers are small, low-cost computers designed to perform specific functions within a larger system. They integrate a processor core, memory (RAM, ROM/Flash), and programmable input/output peripherals on a single chip. Unlike microprocessors, which are the central processing unit of a general-purpose computer, microcontrollers are optimized for control applications and are often found in embedded systems such. Examples include Arduino boards (based on Atmel AVR microcontrollers) and ESP32/ESP8266 modules (based on Espressif SoCs).

The development process for embedded C typically involves several stages: writing the code in an Integrated Development Environment (IDE), compiling the code using a cross-compiler (which translates C code into machine code for the specific microcontroller architecture), linking object files into an executable, and then flashing the executable onto the microcontroller's non-volatile memory (e.g., Flash memory) using a programmer or bootloader. The Arduino IDE, for instance, simplifies this process by integrating a text editor, compiler (AVR-GCC or ESP-IDF based toolchain), and an upload tool.

Controlling GPIO pins is fundamental to embedded programming. Each GPIO pin on a microcontroller can be configured as either an input or an output. When configured as an output, the pin can be set to a HIGH (typically 3.3V or 5V) or LOW (0V) state, allowing it to drive external components like LEDs, relays, or buzzers. To prevent damage to the microcontroller and LED, a current-limiting resistor is always used in series with an LED. The value of this resistor can be calculated using Ohm's Law (R = (Vs - Vf) / I), where Vs is the supply voltage, Vf is the forward voltage of the LED, and I is the desired current through the LED. Understanding how to manipulate these pins through code, often by directly writing to memory-mapped registers or using simplified library functions (like `pinMode()`, `digitalWrite()` in Arduino), is crucial for any embedded system designer. The concept of creating delays, either blocking (using `delay()` functions) or non-blocking (using timers or `millis()` in Arduino), is also essential for timing control in embedded applications. For this experiment, we will focus on basic blocking delays to illustrate LED blinking.","requirements":"Arduino Uno board, USB A-to-B cable, LED (any color), 220 Ohm resistor, Breadboard, Jumper wires.","softwareRequired":"Arduino IDE (latest version).","hardwareRequired":"Arduino Uno, LED, 220 Ohm Resistor, Breadboard, Jumper Wires, USB Cable.","algorithm":"1. Start Arduino IDE.
2. Connect Arduino Uno to the computer via USB cable.
3. Open a new sketch.
4. Define the LED pin.
5. In the setup() function, configure the LED pin as an OUTPUT.
6. In the loop() function, set the LED pin HIGH.
7. Introduce a delay of 1000 milliseconds.
8. Set the LED pin LOW.
9. Introduce another delay of 1000 milliseconds.
10. Compile and upload the program to the Arduino board.
11. Observe the LED blinking.","procedure":["Connect the Arduino Uno board to your computer using the USB cable.","Open the Arduino IDE. Go to 'Tools' -> 'Board' and select 'Arduino Uno'. Then, go to 'Tools' -> 'Port' and select the appropriate COM port for your Arduino.","Construct the circuit: Insert the LED into the breadboard. Connect the long leg (anode) of the LED to one end of the 220 Ohm resistor. Connect the other end of the resistor to Arduino digital pin 13. Connect the short leg (cathode) of the LED to the Arduino GND pin. (Note: Pin 13 usually has a built-in LED, so you can test without an external LED initially, but using an external one is good practice).","Copy the provided Embedded C code into the Arduino IDE's sketch window.","Click the 'Verify' button (checkmark icon) to compile the code and check for errors.","Click the 'Upload' button (right-arrow icon) to compile and flash the program onto the Arduino Uno board.","Observe the LED connected to digital pin 13. It should blink on and off at a one-second interval.","Experiment by changing the delay values in the code and re-uploading to see the effect on the blinking speed."],"program":"```cpp
// Experiment 1: LED Blinking using Arduino (Embedded C)

// Define the digital pin where the LED is connected
const int ledPin = 13; // Most Arduino boards have an on-board LED connected to digital pin 13

// The setup function runs once when you press reset or power the board
void setup() {
  // Initialize digital pin 'ledPin' as an output.
  pinMode(ledPin, OUTPUT);
}

// The loop function runs over and over again forever
void loop() {
  digitalWrite(ledPin, HIGH);   // Turn the LED on (HIGH is the voltage level)
  delay(1000);                  // Wait for a second (1000 milliseconds)
  digitalWrite(ledPin, LOW);    // Turn the LED off by making the voltage LOW
  delay(1000);                  // Wait for a second
}
```","sampleInput":"No direct input. The program executes automatically upon upload.","sampleOutput":"The LED connected to digital pin 13 will turn ON for 1 second, then turn OFF for 1 second, and this cycle will repeat continuously.","observations":"Note the blinking frequency of the LED. Observe how changing the `delay()` values affects the ON and OFF times. Confirm that the circuit connections are stable and the resistor is correctly placed in series with the LED.","result":"Successfully developed and executed an Embedded C program to control a microcontroller's GPIO pin, demonstrating basic output control and understanding of the microcontroller programming workflow.","vivaQuestions":[{"question":"What is the primary difference between a microcontroller and a microprocessor?","answer":"A microcontroller is a compact integrated circuit designed to govern a specific operation in an embedded system, typically including a CPU, memory, and I/O peripherals on a single chip. A microprocessor is only the CPU; it requires external components like RAM, ROM, and I/O to function as a computer."},{"question":"Why is Embedded C preferred over assembly language for most embedded systems development today?","answer":"Embedded C offers higher-level abstraction, better portability across different microcontrollers, improved readability and maintainability, and faster development cycles compared to assembly language, while still providing close-to-hardware access."},{"question":"What is the purpose of the `pinMode()` function in Arduino C?","answer":"The `pinMode()` function is used to configure a specific digital pin on the Arduino board to behave either as an input or an output."},{"question":"Explain the role of the current-limiting resistor used with an LED.","answer":"A current-limiting resistor prevents excessive current from flowing through the LED, which could damage both the LED and the microcontroller's output pin. It ensures the LED operates within its safe current limits."},{"question":"What happens if you omit the `delay()` function in the LED blinking program?","answer":"Without `delay()`, the LED would toggle between HIGH and LOW states so rapidly that it would appear to be continuously ON to the human eye due to persistence of vision, as the switching frequency would be very high."},{"question":"What is the `setup()` function used for in an Arduino sketch?","answer":"The `setup()` function is called once when the sketch starts after powering up or resetting the Arduino board. It is typically used for initializing pin modes, starting serial communication, and other one-time configurations."},{"question":"What is the `loop()` function used for in an Arduino sketch?","answer":"The `loop()` function, as its name suggests, loops consecutively and allows your program to change and respond. It contains the main logic of your program that needs to run continuously."},{"question":"What does 'flashing' a program mean in embedded systems?","answer":"Flashing refers to the process of writing the compiled machine code (firmware) into the non-volatile memory (usually Flash memory) of a microcontroller or embedded device, making it permanently available for execution upon power-up."},{"question":"Why is `volatile` keyword important in embedded programming, especially when dealing with hardware registers or interrupts?","answer":"The `volatile` keyword tells the compiler that a variable's value can change at any time without any action from the code in which it appears. This prevents the compiler from optimizing away reads/writes to this variable, which is crucial for interacting with hardware registers or variables modified by Interrupt Service Routines (ISRs)."},{"question":"What is a 'sketch' in the context of Arduino programming?","answer":"A 'sketch' is the name that Arduino uses for a program. It is a text file containing C/C++ code, saved with the `.ino` file extension, that is compiled and uploaded to the Arduino board."}],"bloomsTaxonomy":"K2 - Understand","coMapping":"CO1","precautions":"1. Ensure the LED is connected with the correct polarity (anode to positive, cathode to ground via resistor).
2. Use the correct value of the current-limiting resistor (typically 220 Ohm to 1 kOhm for a standard LED with a 5V supply) to prevent damage.
3. Verify the correct COM port and board selection in the Arduino IDE before uploading the code.","applications":"1. Simple indication lights in consumer electronics (e.g., power indicator).
2. Status indicators in industrial control panels.
3. Basic signaling systems (e.g., traffic light simulations).","learningOutcome":"Students will be able to set up an embedded development environment, write basic Embedded C programs for digital output control, and understand the fundamental workflow of programming microcontrollers.","references":"1. James K Peckol, Embedded Systems – A Contemporary Design Tool, John Weily, 2008.
2. Shibu K V, Introduction to Embedded Systems, Tata McGraw Hill Education Private Limited, 2009.
3. Arduino Official Documentation: www.arduino.cc/reference/en/"},{"experimentNo":2,"title":"Write Basic and arithmetic Programs Using Embedded C.","aim":"To implement fundamental programming constructs, logical operations, and arithmetic calculations using Embedded C on a microcontroller, displaying results via serial communication.","objectives":"- To practice conditional statements (if-else), loops (for, while), and switch-case structures in Embedded C.
- To perform basic arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, modulo) and bitwise operations.
- To utilize serial communication to display program output and debug embedded applications.","theory":"Embedded C programming, while similar to standard C, requires a keen awareness of the microcontroller's limited resources such as memory (RAM and Flash) and processing power. Efficient use of these resources is paramount. Basic programming constructs like conditional statements (`if`, `else if`, `else`), looping constructs (`for`, `while`, `do-while`), and selection statements (`switch-case`) are fundamental for controlling program flow based on specific conditions or for repetitive tasks. These constructs behave similarly to standard C but must be implemented with efficiency in mind to avoid unnecessary memory consumption or execution delays.

Arithmetic operations are straightforward: addition `+`, subtraction `-`, multiplication `*`, division `/`, and modulo `%` (for integer remainders). However, the data types chosen for these operations are critical. Integer types (`int`, `char`, `long`) are common, but their sizes vary across different microcontroller architectures (e.g., `int` might be 16-bit on an AVR or 32-bit on an ARM Cortex-M). Floating-point operations (`float`, `double`) are often computationally expensive and can consume significant Flash memory, so fixed-point arithmetic or scaled integers are sometimes preferred in deeply embedded systems where performance and memory are tight constraints.

Bitwise operations (`&` AND, `|` OR, `^` XOR, `~` NOT, `<<` left shift, `>>` right shift) are exceptionally important in embedded systems. They allow direct manipulation of individual bits within registers, which is how peripherals are configured and controlled at a low level. For example, setting a specific bit in a control register might enable a timer or configure a pin's mode.

Serial communication (UART - Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter) is a common method for microcontrollers to communicate with a host computer or other devices. In Arduino, this is facilitated by the `Serial` object and functions like `Serial.begin()`, `Serial.print()`, and `Serial.println()`. `Serial.begin(baud_rate)` initializes the serial port at a specified baud rate (e.g., 9600, 115200), which dictates the speed of data transfer. `Serial.print()` and `Serial.println()` send data to the serial monitor. This allows for debugging, displaying sensor readings, or interacting with the microcontroller. It is a non-blocking form of communication that is highly useful for conveying information from the embedded system back to the developer without needing complex display hardware.","requirements":"Arduino Uno board, USB A-to-B cable, Computer with Arduino IDE.","softwareRequired":"Arduino IDE (latest version).","hardwareRequired":"Arduino Uno, USB Cable.","algorithm":"1. Initialize serial communication at a specified baud rate.
2. Declare and initialize integer variables for arithmetic operations.
3. Perform addition, subtraction, multiplication, division, and modulo operations.
4. Print the results of arithmetic operations to the serial monitor.
5. Declare variables for bitwise operations.
6. Perform bitwise AND, OR, XOR, NOT, left shift, and right shift operations.
7. Print the results of bitwise operations to the serial monitor.
8. Implement an `if-else` statement to check a condition and print corresponding messages.
9. Implement a `for` loop to print a sequence of numbers.
10. Implement a `while` loop to count up to a limit.
11. Implement a `switch-case` statement based on a variable and print corresponding messages.","procedure":["Connect the Arduino Uno board to your computer via the USB cable.","Open the Arduino IDE. Select the correct board ('Arduino Uno') and COM port from the 'Tools' menu.","Open a new sketch and paste the provided Embedded C code into the editor.","Review the code to understand the implementation of arithmetic, bitwise operations, and control flow statements.","Click the 'Verify' button to compile the code. Correct any syntax errors if present.","Click the 'Upload' button to flash the program onto the Arduino Uno board.","Once uploaded, open the 'Serial Monitor' from 'Tools' -> 'Serial Monitor' or by clicking the icon in the top right corner of the IDE. Ensure the baud rate in the Serial Monitor matches the `Serial.begin()` value in your code (e.g., 9600 baud).","Observe the output displayed in the Serial Monitor, which will show the results of the arithmetic operations, bitwise operations, and messages from the control flow examples.","Experiment by changing the input values for the variables in the code and observing how the output changes in the Serial Monitor."],"program":"```cpp
// Experiment 2: Basic and Arithmetic Programs using Embedded C

void setup() {
  // Initialize serial communication at 9600 bits per second.
  // This allows the Arduino to send data to the Serial Monitor.
  Serial.begin(9600);
  while (!Serial) {
    ; // wait for serial port to connect. Needed for native USB port only
  }
  Serial.println("--- Embedded C Basic & Arithmetic Programs ---");
  Serial.println();
}

void loop() {
  // --- Arithmetic Operations ---
  int a = 10;
  int b = 3;
  int result;

  Serial.println("*** Arithmetic Operations ***");

  result = a + b;
  Serial.print("Addition (a + b): ");
  Serial.println(result); // Expected: 13

  result = a - b;
  Serial.print("Subtraction (a - b): ");
  Serial.println(result); // Expected: 7

  result = a * b;
  Serial.print("Multiplication (a * b): ");
  Serial.println(result); // Expected: 30

  result = a / b;
  Serial.print("Division (a / b): ");
  Serial.println(result); // Expected: 3 (integer division)

  result = a % b;
  Serial.print("Modulo (a % b): ");
  Serial.println(result); // Expected: 1
  Serial.println();

  // --- Bitwise Operations ---
  unsigned char val1 = 0b10101100; // Binary: 172
  unsigned char val2 = 0b01010110; // Binary: 86
  unsigned char bitResult;

  Serial.println("*** Bitwise Operations ***");

  bitResult = val1 & val2;
  Serial.print("Bitwise AND (val1 & val2): 0b");
  Serial.println(bitResult, BIN); // Expected: 0b00000100 (4)

  bitResult = val1 | val2;
  Serial.print("Bitwise OR (val1 | val2):  0b");
  Serial.println(bitResult, BIN); // Expected: 0b11111110 (254)

  bitResult = val1 ^ val2;
  Serial.print("Bitwise XOR (val1 ^ val2): 0b");
  Serial.println(bitResult, BIN); // Expected: 0b11111010 (250)

  bitResult = ~val1; // Bitwise NOT is typically applied to a single value
  Serial.print("Bitwise NOT (~val1):     0b");
  Serial.println(bitResult, BIN); // Expected: 0b01010011 (83 for 8-bit unsigned char)

  bitResult = val1 << 2;
  Serial.print("Left Shift (val1 << 2):  0b");
  Serial.println(bitResult, BIN); // Expected: 0b10110000 (176 if bits are shifted out and zeros fill in)

  bitResult = val2 >> 1;
  Serial.print("Right Shift (val2 >> 1): 0b");
  Serial.println(bitResult, BIN); // Expected: 0b00101011 (43)
  Serial.println();

  // --- Control Flow: if-else ---
  int temperature = 25;
  Serial.println("*** Control Flow: if-else ***");
  if (temperature > 30) {
    Serial.println("It's hot!");
  } else if (temperature < 10) {
    Serial.println("It's cold!");
  } else {
    Serial.println("Temperature is moderate."); // Expected
  }
  Serial.println();

  // --- Control Flow: for loop ---
  Serial.println("*** Control Flow: for loop (Count to 5) ***");
  for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    Serial.print("Count: ");
    Serial.println(i);
  }
  Serial.println();

  // --- Control Flow: while loop ---
  Serial.println("*** Control Flow: while loop (Countdown from 3) ***");
  int countdown = 3;
  while (countdown > 0) {
    Serial.print("Countdown: ");
    Serial.println(countdown);
    countdown--;
  }
  Serial.println("Lift off!");
  Serial.println();

  // --- Control Flow: switch-case ---
  char command = 'B'; // Try 'A', 'B', 'C', or 'X'
  Serial.print("*** Control Flow: switch-case for command '");
  Serial.print(command);
  Serial.println("' ***");
  switch (command) {
    case 'A':
      Serial.println("Command A received.");
      break;
    case 'B':
      Serial.println("Command B received."); // Expected
      break;
    case 'C':
      Serial.println("Command C received.");
      break;
    default:
      Serial.println("Unknown command.");
      break;
  }
  Serial.println();

  delay(10000); // Delay for 10 seconds to allow reading output before loop repeats
}
```","sampleInput":"No direct user input. The program uses predefined variable values for demonstration. For interactive testing, one could modify `int temperature` or `char command` variables in the code and re-upload.","sampleOutput":"The Serial Monitor will display the results of various operations and control flow executions:

```
--- Embedded C Basic & Arithmetic Programs ---

*** Arithmetic Operations ***
Addition (a + b): 13
Subtraction (a - b): 7
Multiplication (a * b): 30
Division (a / b): 3
Modulo (a % b): 1

*** Bitwise Operations ***
Bitwise AND (val1 & val2): 0b00000100
Bitwise OR (val1 | val2):  0b11111110
Bitwise XOR (val1 ^ val2): 0b11111010
Bitwise NOT (~val1):     0b01010011
Left Shift (val1 << 2):  0b10110000
Right Shift (val2 >> 1): 0b00101011

*** Control Flow: if-else ***
Temperature is moderate.

*** Control Flow: for loop (Count to 5) ***
Count: 1
Count: 2
Count: 3
Count: 4
