3. Adafruit DHT Sensor Library Documentation: learn.adafruit.com/dht-humidity-sensing-on-raspberry-pi-with-gpython/using-the-dht-sensor"},{"experimentNo":9,"title":"Develop a program to display temperature and humidity readings in LCD.","aim":"To interface a DHT11/DHT22 temperature and humidity sensor with an Arduino microcontroller and display the real-time readings on a 16x2 I2C Liquid Crystal Display (LCD).","objectives":"- To correctly wire a DHT sensor and an I2C LCD module to an Arduino Uno.
- To install and utilize the necessary libraries for DHT sensor reading and I2C LCD control.
- To write an Embedded C program that reads environmental data and displays it clearly on the LCD.","theory":"Displaying sensor data locally is a common requirement in embedded systems, providing immediate feedback to users without needing a computer or network connection. This experiment combines sensor interfacing with display technology to create a standalone environmental monitor.

**DHT11/DHT22 Sensor:** As introduced previously, these digital sensors are cost-effective and popular choices for measuring ambient temperature and relative humidity. They provide digital output, making them easy to interface with microcontrollers. The DHT11 is typically less accurate and has a smaller range than the DHT22, but both operate on a similar single-wire data protocol. A pull-up resistor (4.7kΩ to 10kΩ) is typically required on the data line to ensure proper communication.

**16x2 I2C LCD Module:** A 16x2 LCD is a standard character display capable of showing 16 characters per line across two lines. Interfacing a standard parallel 16x2 LCD directly to a microcontroller can consume a significant number of GPIO pins (typically 6 to 11 pins). To mitigate this, an I2C LCD module is often used. This module incorporates an I2C communication chip (like the PCF8574) which translates I2C commands into parallel commands for the LCD. This reduces the required microcontroller pins to just two for data (SDA) and clock (SCL), plus power (VCC, GND), making it highly efficient for pin-constrained projects. The I2C protocol allows multiple I2C devices to share the same two bus lines, further enhancing pin efficiency.

**Software Libraries:** Arduino's rich ecosystem of libraries simplifies complex tasks. For the DHT sensor, the Adafruit DHT Unified Sensor library (or similar) provides functions to initialize the sensor and read temperature/humidity values. For the I2C LCD, the `LiquidCrystal_I2C` library provides easy-to-use functions for initialization, printing text, setting cursor position, and controlling the backlight. These libraries abstract away the low-level register manipulations and communication protocols, allowing developers to focus on application logic.

This experiment demonstrates a complete local data acquisition and display system, crucial for many standalone embedded applications.","requirements":"Arduino Uno board, USB A-to-B cable, DHT11 or DHT22 Temperature & Humidity Sensor, 4.7k-10k Ohm resistor (for DHT sensor), 16x2 I2C LCD Module, Breadboard, Jumper wires, Computer with Arduino IDE.","softwareRequired":"Arduino IDE (latest version), `DHT sensor library` by Adafruit, `LiquidCrystal_I2C` library by Frank de Brabander.","hardwareRequired":"Arduino Uno, DHT11/DHT22 Sensor, 4.7k-10k Ohm Resistor, 16x2 I2C LCD Module, Breadboard, Jumper Wires, USB Cable.","algorithm":"1. Define DHT sensor pin and type, and LCD I2C address and dimensions.
2. In `setup()`:
    a. Initialize serial communication for debugging.
    b. Initialize the DHT sensor.
    c. Initialize the I2C LCD module and turn on its backlight.
    d. Print a welcome message to the LCD.
3. In `loop()`:
    a. Read temperature and humidity data from the DHT sensor.
    b. Check if the readings are valid. If not, print an error message to Serial Monitor.
    c. If valid:
        i. Clear the first line of the LCD.
        ii. Set the cursor to (0,0) and print "Temp: XX.XX C".
        iii. Clear the second line of the LCD.
        iv. Set the cursor to (0,1) and print "Hum : YY.YY %".
    d. Introduce a delay (e.g., 2 seconds) before the next reading/update.","procedure":["Connect the Arduino Uno board to your computer via the USB cable and open the Arduino IDE. Select the correct board ('Arduino Uno') and COM port from the 'Tools' menu.","**DHT Sensor Wiring**: Connect DHT VCC to Arduino 5V, DHT GND to Arduino GND. Connect the DHT data pin to Arduino digital pin 4. Add a 4.7k-10k Ohm pull-up resistor between the DHT data pin and VCC.","**I2C LCD Wiring**: Connect LCD VCC to Arduino 5V, LCD GND to Arduino GND. Connect LCD SDA to Arduino A4 (SDA pin on Uno). Connect LCD SCL to Arduino A5 (SCL pin on Uno).","**Install Libraries**: Go to 'Sketch' -> 'Include Library' -> 'Manage Libraries...'. Search for 'DHT sensor library' by Adafruit and install it. Then search for 'LiquidCrystal I2C' by Frank de Brabander and install it.","Copy the provided Embedded C code into a new Arduino sketch. Ensure the correct I2C address for your LCD module (usually 0x27 or 0x3F).","Compile and upload the code to your Arduino Uno board.","Open the Serial Monitor (baud rate 9600) to observe debug messages and raw sensor readings.","Observe the 16x2 I2C LCD. After a brief initialization message, it should display the current temperature on the first line and humidity on the second line, updating periodically (e.g., every 2 seconds).","Experiment by changing the refresh interval or the displayed format on the LCD.","Gently blow warm/moist air onto the DHT sensor to observe changes in readings on the LCD."],"program":"```cpp
// Experiment 9: Display Temperature and Humidity Readings on LCD

// --- DHT Sensor Libraries ---
#include "DHT.h"      // For DHT sensor library
#define DHTPIN 4      // DHT sensor connected to digital pin 4
#define DHTTYPE DHT11 // DHT 11 or DHT22

DHT dht(DHTPIN, DHTTYPE);

// --- I2C LCD Libraries ---
#include <Wire.h> // Required for I2C communication
#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h> // Library for I2C LCD

// Set the LCD address to 0x27 or 0x3F (check your specific module)
LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(0x27, 16, 2); // LCD address, 16 columns and 2 rows

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  while (!Serial) {
    ; // wait for serial port to connect
  }
  Serial.println("--- Temperature & Humidity on I2C LCD ---");

  dht.begin(); // Initialize DHT sensor
  Serial.println("DHT sensor initialized.");

  lcd.init();   // Initialize the LCD
  if (!lcd.begin(16, 2)) {
    Serial.println("LCD initialization failed! Check wiring/address.");
    while (true);
  }
  lcd.backlight(); // Turn on backlight
  lcd.clear();
  lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  lcd.print("Initializing...");
  Serial.println("I2C LCD initialized.");
  delay(2000);
}

void loop() {
  // Wait a few seconds between measurements.
  delay(2000); // Read every 2 seconds

  // Reading temperature or humidity takes about 250 milliseconds!
  // Sensor readings may also be up to 2 seconds 'old' (its a very slow sensor)
  float h = dht.readHumidity();
  // Read temperature as Celsius (the default)
  float t = dht.readTemperature();

  // Check if any reads failed and exit early (to try again next loop iteration)
  if (isnan(h) || isnan(t)) {
    Serial.println("Failed to read from DHT sensor!");
    lcd.clear();
    lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
    lcd.print("Sensor Error!");
    lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
    lcd.print("Check Wiring");
    return;
  }

  // Print to Serial Monitor for debugging
  Serial.print("Humidity: ");
  Serial.print(h);
  Serial.print(" %\t");
  Serial.print("Temperature: ");
  Serial.print(t);
  Serial.println(" *C");

  // Display on LCD
  lcd.clear();
  lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  lcd.print("Temp: ");
  lcd.print(t);
  lcd.print(" C");

  lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcd.print("Hum : ");
  lcd.print(h);
  lcd.print(" %");
}
