{"experiments":[{"experimentNo":1,"title":"Exploring Microcontroller-Based Programs Using Embedded C","aim":"To understand the fundamental architecture and programming concepts of microcontrollers using Embedded C language.","objectives":"• To configure the development environment for embedded C programming.
• To write, compile, and upload basic Embedded C programs to a microcontroller.
• To observe the execution of simple microcontroller-based applications.","theory":"Microcontrollers are small, integrated circuits designed to govern a specific operation in an embedded system. They are essentially mini-computers on a single chip, comprising a processor core, memory (both program and data), and programmable input/output peripherals. Unlike microprocessors, which are general-purpose processors requiring external components to form a functional system, microcontrollers integrate all necessary components for a small computing system directly onto a single die. This integration reduces system complexity, power consumption, and cost, making them ideal for embedded applications.

Key components of a microcontroller include: 
1. Central Processing Unit (CPU): The brain of the microcontroller, responsible for executing instructions.
2. Program Memory (Flash/ROM): Stores the embedded C program instructions, typically non-volatile.
3. Data Memory (RAM): Used for temporary storage of data during program execution.
4. Input/Output (I/O) Ports: Pins that can be configured as inputs or outputs to interface with external devices like LEDs, buttons, sensors, and actuators.
5. Timers/Counters: Used for precise timing, generating delays, measuring pulse widths, and counting external events.
6. Analog-to-Digital Converters (ADCs): Convert analog signals from sensors into digital values that the CPU can process.
7. Communication Interfaces: Protocols like UART (Universal Asynchronous Receiver-Transmitter), SPI (Serial Peripheral Interface), I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit), and CAN (Controller Area Network) allow the microcontroller to communicate with other devices.

Embedded C is a set of extensions to the C language, specifically designed for programming microcontrollers and embedded systems. While standard C can be used, Embedded C provides additional features to deal with the unique requirements of embedded hardware, such as direct access to hardware registers, fixed-point arithmetic, and interrupt handling. Key aspects of Embedded C programming include:

• Register-level Programming: Microcontroller peripherals are controlled by writing to or reading from specific hardware registers. Embedded C allows direct manipulation of these registers using pointers and bitwise operations.
• Memory Mapping: Understanding how memory is organized (program memory, data memory, I/O memory) is crucial for efficient embedded programming.
• Interrupt Service Routines (ISRs): These are special functions that execute automatically when a specific hardware event (an interrupt) occurs, allowing the system to respond quickly to external stimuli.
• Volatile Keyword: Used for variables that might be changed by an external factor (like an interrupt service routine) outside the normal flow of program execution, preventing the compiler from optimizing away reads of such variables.
• Fixed-Point Arithmetic: Often used in resource-constrained environments where floating-point operations are computationally expensive or not supported by the hardware.

Developing embedded C programs typically involves an Integrated Development Environment (IDE) that includes a compiler, linker, and debugger specific to the target microcontroller architecture. The compiler translates the C source code into machine-readable instructions. The linker combines compiled code with necessary libraries. Finally, the program is uploaded to the microcontroller's flash memory, and its execution can be observed, often via serial communication or by monitoring the behavior of connected peripherals. This hands-on exploration provides a foundational understanding necessary for more complex embedded system design.","requirements":"Hardware: Arduino UNO development board, USB A-B Cable, Breadboard, LED (Light Emitting Diode), 220 Ohm Resistor, Jumper Wires. Software: Arduino IDE (Integrated Development Environment).","softwareRequired":"Arduino IDE (Integrated Development Environment)","hardwareRequired":"Arduino UNO development board, USB A-B Cable, Breadboard, LED (Light Emitting Diode), 220 Ohm Resistor, Jumper Wires","algorithm":"1. Initialize the Arduino IDE and select the correct board and port.
