8. Repeat steps 4-7 indefinitely.","procedure":["1. Install the Arduino IDE on your computer if you haven't already. Download it from the official Arduino website.","2. Connect the Arduino UNO board to your computer using the USB A-B cable.","3. Open the Arduino IDE. Go to 'Tools' -> 'Board' and select 'Arduino Uno'.","4. Go to 'Tools' -> 'Port' and select the serial port corresponding to your Arduino board (it might be COMx on Windows or /dev/ttyUSBx or /dev/cu.usbmodemx on Linux/macOS).","5. Connect the long leg (anode) of the LED to Digital Pin 13 of the Arduino UNO via a 220 Ohm resistor. Connect the short leg (cathode) of the LED to the GND pin of the Arduino. (Digital Pin 13 usually has a built-in resistor and LED, but using an external one is good practice).","6. Copy the provided Embedded C code into the Arduino IDE editor.","7. Click the 'Verify' button (checkmark icon) to compile the code and check for errors.","8. If compilation is successful, click the 'Upload' button (right arrow icon) to upload the compiled program to your Arduino UNO board.","9. Observe the LED connected to Pin 13; it should start blinking, turning ON and OFF with a specified delay.","10. Experiment with changing the delay values in the code and re-uploading to see the effect on the blink speed."],"program":"void setup() {
  // Initialize digital pin 13 as an output.
  pinMode(13, OUTPUT);
}

// The loop function runs over and over again forever
void loop() {
  digitalWrite(13, HIGH);   // turn the LED on (HIGH is the voltage level)
  delay(1000);              // wait for a second (1000 milliseconds)
  digitalWrite(13, LOW);    // turn the LED off by making the voltage LOW
  delay(1000);              // wait for a second
}","sampleInput":"The program runs continuously on the Arduino board. No direct user input is required during runtime for this specific program. The 'input' is the compiled code uploaded to the microcontroller.","sampleOutput":"The LED connected to Digital Pin 13 on the Arduino UNO board will blink. It will turn ON for 1 second, then OFF for 1 second, and this cycle will repeat indefinitely.","observations":"Note the blinking frequency of the LED. Observe if the LED turns on and off consistently. Verify that modifying the `delay()` values in the code directly impacts the blinking speed and duration.","result":"The fundamental concepts of microcontroller programming using Embedded C were successfully explored by implementing a basic LED blinking program on an Arduino UNO board. The program compiled, uploaded, and executed as expected, demonstrating control over a digital output pin.","vivaQuestions":[{"question":"What is an embedded system?","answer":"An embedded system is a computer system with a dedicated function within a larger mechanical or electrical system, often with real-time computing constraints. It is embedded as part of a complete device."},{"question":"Differentiate between a microcontroller and a microprocessor.","answer":"A microcontroller integrates CPU, memory (RAM, ROM/Flash), and I/O peripherals on a single chip, making it a self-contained system. A microprocessor is just a CPU that requires external components like memory, I/O controllers, and other peripherals to function as a complete system."},{"question":"Why is Embedded C preferred for microcontroller programming?","answer":"Embedded C is preferred due to its close-to-hardware access capabilities, efficiency, small code size, predictable execution time, and extensions for handling specific hardware features like interrupts and I/O ports, which are crucial for embedded systems."},{"question":"What is the role of the `pinMode()` function in Arduino C?","answer":"The `pinMode()` function is used to configure a specific pin to behave either as an input or an output. For example, `pinMode(13, OUTPUT)` configures pin 13 to send electrical signals."},{"question":"Explain the purpose of the `digitalWrite()` function.","answer":"The `digitalWrite()` function is used to write a HIGH (5V or 3.3V, depending on board) or LOW (0V) value to a digital pin. This is typically used to turn devices like LEDs on or off, or send digital signals to other components."},{"question":"What does `delay()` function do?","answer":"The `delay()` function pauses the program for a specified number of milliseconds. It's a blocking function, meaning no other code will execute during the delay period."},{"question":"What is the `setup()` function in Arduino programming?","answer":"The `setup()` function is called once when the sketch starts after power-up or reset. It's used to initialize variables, pin modes, start serial communication, and include libraries."},{"question":"What is the `loop()` function in Arduino programming?","answer":"The `loop()` function, as its name suggests, loops consecutively and allows your program to change and respond. It executes repeatedly after the `setup()` function completes."},{"question":"What is a 'sketch' in the context of Arduino?","answer":"A 'sketch' is the term for a program written for Arduino. It is the unit of code that is uploaded to and run on the Arduino board."},{"question":"How do you connect a current-limiting resistor with an LED?","answer":"A current-limiting resistor is connected in series with the LED. It limits the current flowing through the LED to prevent it from burning out, as LEDs have a low forward voltage and draw too much current directly from the microcontroller pin."}],"bloomsTaxonomy":"K2 - Understand","coMapping":"CO1","precautions":"1. Always ensure correct polarity when connecting LEDs (long leg to positive/resistor, short leg to ground) to avoid damaging them.
