7. Print the results of bitwise operations to the serial monitor.
8. Ensure to handle potential integer overflow for smaller data types.","procedure":["1. Open the Arduino IDE on your computer.","2. Connect your Arduino UNO board to the computer using the USB cable.","3. Select the correct board type ('Arduino Uno') and serial port under the 'Tools' menu in the Arduino IDE.","4. Copy the provided Embedded C code into a new sketch in the Arduino IDE.","5. Review the code to understand the various arithmetic and bitwise operations being performed.","6. Compile the code by clicking the 'Verify' button (checkmark icon). Address any compilation errors.","7. Upload the compiled code to your Arduino board by clicking the 'Upload' button (right arrow icon).","8. Open the Serial Monitor in the Arduino IDE (Tools -> Serial Monitor or Ctrl+Shift+M).","9. Set the baud rate of the Serial Monitor to 9600 (or whatever is specified in `Serial.begin()`) to match the program's output.","10. Observe the output displayed in the Serial Monitor, which will show the results of the arithmetic and bitwise operations.","11. Experiment by changing the input values for the variables and observe how the output changes after re-uploading the code."],"program":"#include <stdint.h> // For fixed-width integer types

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600); // Initialize serial communication at 9600 bits per second
