 is applied. A passive buzzer requires an external oscillating signal (e.g., a square wave from a microcontroller) to produce sound, allowing for variable pitch control."},{"question":"What is the purpose of `pinMode(pin, OUTPUT)`?","answer":"The `pinMode(pin, OUTPUT)` function in Arduino configures a specified digital pin to function as an output. This means the microcontroller can send voltage (HIGH or LOW) through that pin to control external devices."},{"question":"What are the common values for `digitalWrite()` and what do they represent?","answer":"The common values for `digitalWrite()` are `HIGH` and `LOW`. `HIGH` typically represents the supply voltage (e.g., 5V for Arduino Uno, 3.3V for Raspberry Pi), turning a device ON. `LOW` represents 0V (ground), turning a device OFF."},{"question":"Explain the role of `delay()` in Arduino programming.","answer":"The `delay(milliseconds)` function pauses the program's execution for the specified number of milliseconds. It is a blocking function, meaning no other instructions will be executed until the delay period is over."},{"question":"How do you control a GPIO pin in Raspberry Pi using Python?","answer":"In Raspberry Pi Python, you use the `RPi.GPIO` library. First, import `RPi.GPIO as GPIO` and set the pin numbering mode (`GPIO.setmode`). Then, configure the pin direction (`GPIO.setup(pin, GPIO.OUT)`) and control its state (`GPIO.output(pin, GPIO.HIGH/LOW)`)."},{"question":"What are the two common pin numbering schemes for Raspberry Pi GPIOs?","answer":"The two common pin numbering schemes are `GPIO.BOARD` (referencing physical pin numbers on the header) and `GPIO.BCM` (referencing Broadcom chip-specific GPIO numbers)."},{"question":"What is the function of `GPIO.cleanup()` in Raspberry Pi Python?","answer":"The `GPIO.cleanup()` function is used to reset all the GPIO channels that have been set up in the script back to their default state (inputs with no pull-up/pull-down resistors). It's good practice to call it at the end of your script, especially in `finally` blocks."},{"question":"How can you make an LED blink at a different rate without changing `delay()` values symmetrically?","answer":"To make an LED blink at a different rate, you can provide different `delay()` values for the ON and OFF states. For example, `digitalWrite(LED, HIGH); delay(100); digitalWrite(LED, LOW); delay(1000);` would make it flash briefly and stay off longer."}],"bloomsTaxonomy":"K3 - Apply","coMapping":"CO4","precautions":"1. Always use a current-limiting resistor with LEDs to prevent damage to the LED and the microcontroller pin.
