 equal steps. Its main advantages are precise positional control, good holding torque at standstill, and repeatable accuracy, making it suitable for applications like 3D printers and CNC machines."},{"question":"Explain the difference between a unipolar and bipolar stepper motor.","answer":"Unipolar stepper motors have one coil with a center tap per phase, allowing current to flow in only one direction. Bipolar stepper motors have a single coil per phase, requiring current to be reversed, which necessitates a more complex driver but provides higher torque efficiency."},{"question":"What is debouncing, and why is it important for push buttons?","answer":"Debouncing is the process of removing spurious, rapid transitions (noise) that occur when a mechanical switch is pressed or released due to contact bounce. It's important for push buttons to ensure that a single physical press registers as only one logical event, preventing multiple false triggers."},{"question":"How can you control the speed of a DC motor using an L293D driver?","answer":"The speed of a DC motor with an L293D can be controlled by applying a Pulse Width Modulation (PWM) signal to the L293D's enable pin (EN). Varying the duty cycle of the PWM signal changes the average voltage supplied to the motor, thus controlling its speed."},{"question":"What is the purpose of `INPUT_PULLUP` when configuring a button pin?","answer":"`INPUT_PULLUP` configures a digital pin as an input and enables an internal pull-up resistor. This means the pin will be HIGH by default when the button is not pressed, and LOW when the button is pressed (connecting it to ground), simplifying wiring by eliminating the need for an external pull-up resistor."},{"question":"What is the common use case for a 28BYJ-48 stepper motor with a ULN2003 driver?","answer":"The 28BYJ-48 stepper motor is a small, inexpensive unipolar stepper motor often used with a ULN2003 Darlington array driver for hobbyist projects, simple robotic arms, valve control, or other applications requiring precise, low-torque positioning."},{"question":"How do you reverse the direction of a DC motor using an L293D?","answer":"To reverse the direction of a DC motor connected to an L293D, you simply swap the HIGH and LOW signals on its two input pins (e.g., if IN1 was HIGH and IN2 was LOW for forward, set IN1 to LOW and IN2 to HIGH for reverse)."}],"bloomsTaxonomy":"K3 - Apply","coMapping":"CO4","precautions":"1. Always use an external power supply for motors, separate from the microcontroller's power supply, and ensure their grounds are common to prevent damage to the microcontroller due to motor current spikes.
